## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a practical on-device data structure with incremental updates, offline-first UX, and a clear 12‑month cost plan, including privacy considerations.

## Key Concepts

- Local inverted index and incremental updates
- Offline-first data paths and cache invalidation
- Delta-based cloud sync and conflict resolution
- Resource budgeting: storage, CPU, memory, latency, energy
- Privacy, compliance, and user consent handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal incremental inverted index (conceptual)
class InvertedIndex {
  constructor() {
    this.index = {}; // term -> Set of note IDs
  }
  addNote(id, text) {
    text.split(/\W+/).forEach(t => {
      if (!t) return;
      const w = t.toLowerCase();
      (this.index[w] ||= new Set()).add(id);
    });
  }
  search(q) {
    return q
      .toLowerCase()
      .split(/\s+/)
      .flatMap(w => Array.from(this.index[w] || []));
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you estimate index maintenance energy and storage growth over time?
- How would you handle search latency guarantees on mid-range devices with thousands of notes?
- What privacy safeguards would you implement for on-device indexing and sync deltas?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical understanding of edge caching systems, cost modeling, and operational considerations for beginner-friendly distributed systems design.

## Key Concepts

- Edge caching strategies and TTL-based invalidation
- Delta synchronization for bandwidth optimization
- Energy-aware system design for battery-powered devices
- Phased rollout planning with cohort analysis
- Offline-first architecture and fallback behavior
- Privacy, compliance, and regulatory considerations

## Code Example

```javascript
function needsRefresh(lastRefresh, ttlHours) {
  const now = Date.now();
  const ttlMs = ttlHours * 60 * 60 * 1000;
  return (now - lastRefresh) >= ttlMs;
}

function getRefreshStrategy(device) {
  const batteryLevel = device.battery ?? 100;
  const isCharging = device.isCharging ?? false;

  if (batteryLevel < 20 && !isCharging) {
    return 'defer';
  } else if (batteryLevel < 50) {
    return 'adaptive';
  }
  return 'standard';
}
```

